{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "travel_policy_risk_flags",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Directly targets the query by categorizing specific travel policy risks (air travel, business class, high-value, luxury, international), giving a rich axis to group and prioritize expenses. However, the evidence samples are all hardware/cloud IT asset records with no travel content, yet the taxonomy asserts confident policy-specific inferences ('requires VP approval', '>$5,000 requires manager approval') that are not derivable from the visible source fields (short_description, ci); amount is an original column so high-value flags could be grounded, but the specific travel categorizations are not evidenced in the samples and risk over-asserting. The dominant 'No travel policy risks' value is a reasonable silent-row reading."
    },
    {
      "name": "travel_policy_risk_level",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A Low/Medium/High severity ordinal is a useful, distinct axis for prioritizing which expenses need review, more than a bare count. It is largely derived from the flags column, so partly redundant but adds an interpretable severity dimension. Groundedness is limited by the same underlying flag inferences, but NULL/Low for the non-travel sample rows is correct silent-row behavior."
    },
    {
      "name": "travel_policy_risk_count",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.35,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A count of flags is largely a mechanical restatement of the flags column and correlates almost perfectly with risk_level, offering little independent analytical leverage for identifying policy risks. It is well-grounded as a direct derivation from the flags, and 0 for non-travel rows is correct."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
  "source_groundedness": 0.5,
  "rationale": "The flags column is well-targeted to the query and provides a genuine categorical axis; the risk_level adds an ordinal severity view, while risk_count is mostly redundant. The main concern is groundedness: all provided evidence rows are IT hardware/cloud asset records with no travel content, and the taxonomy embeds confident, policy-specific approval assertions whose derivability from the visible source text is unclear, though the silent-row 'No risk'/NULL/0 defaults are handled correctly."
}

